WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.680
A local slowdown is evidence, not an end-to-end
forecast.

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:09.748
A shorter parallel branch can slow without changing
the join time, while a heavily used shared

00:00:09.748 --> 00:00:12.072
resource can amplify a small change.

00:00:12.072 --> 00:00:18.205
Lizhi’s approach combines early local measurements
with an architectural model so we can reason

00:00:18.205 --> 00:00:20.594
about workload, paths and contention.

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:25.831
Measure original and updated code under comparable
conditions and repeat the runs.

00:00:25.831 --> 00:00:30.807
The lecture combines a distribution test, Cliff’s
delta and a mean difference.

00:00:30.807 --> 00:00:35.081
Statistical evidence does not tell us the whole-
system consequence.

00:00:35.081 --> 00:00:41.142
Keep the protocol and uncertainty visible, and avoid
calling a noisy or mismatched experiment a

00:00:41.142 --> 00:00:41.844
regression.

00:00:41.844 --> 00:00:46.654
Map the local deviation to the modeled activity
before updating a parameter.

00:00:46.654 --> 00:00:51.844
The lecture illustrates graph matching and
propagation through the call structure.

00:00:51.844 --> 00:00:57.097
Keep units and measurement boundaries clear, and
avoid double counting nested work.

00:00:57.097 --> 00:01:03.046
Compare original and changed models across
workloads, then validate predictions independently.

00:01:03.046 --> 00:01:07.476
Experimental success in selected systems is not a
universal guarantee.
